   "title": "A loop and a template literal",
   "blurb": "A while loop and `${...}` interpolation. Watch the template literal turn into a printf format string plus arguments.",
   "file": "examples/intro/fibonacci.yoop",
   "source": "// Variables, a loop, and string interpolation.\n//\n// `let` introduces a mutable binding; the `: int32` annotation is\n// optional when the initializer makes the type obvious, but spelled out\n// here for clarity. Backtick strings are template literals - `${expr}`\n// splices a value into the text, the same idea as JavaScript.\n\nfunction main(): int32 {\n    let a: int32 = 0;\n    let b: int32 = 1;\n    let i: int32 = 0;\n\n    // Print the first ten Fibonacci numbers.\n    while (i < 10) {\n        printf(`fib(${i}) = ${a}\\n`);\n        let next: int32 = a + b;\n        a = b;\n        b = next;\n        i = i + 1;\n    }\n\n    return 0;\n}\n\n// expected output:\n// fib(0) = 0\n// fib(1) = 1\n// fib(2) = 1\n// fib(3) = 2\n// fib(4) = 3\n// fib(5) = 5\n// fib(6) = 8\n// fib(7) = 13\n// fib(8) = 21\n// fib(9) = 34\n",
